## 5. Expressions

### Operators

| Operator | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `-x` | negation |
| `{{.Not}} x` | logical not |
| `a * b` | multiplication |
| `a / b` | integer division (see below) |
| `a {{.ModOp}} b` | remainder (see below) |
| `a + b` | addition{{if eq .Concat "+"}}, or joining text (see below){{end}} |
| `a - b` | subtraction |
{{- if ne .Concat "+"}}
| `a {{.Concat}} b` | joining text (see below) |
{{- end}}
| `a {{.Eq}} b` | equal |
| `a {{.Ne}} b` | not equal |
| `a < b`, `a <= b`, `a > b`, `a >= b` | ordering |
| `a {{.And}} b` | logical and |
| `a {{.Or}} b` | logical or |

Note the spellings: assignment is `{{.Assign}}`{{if ne .Assign .Eq}} and equality is `{{.Eq}}`{{end}}.
{{- if eq .Assign "<-"}} Because `<-` is one token, write `a < -1` with a space
when comparing with a negative number.{{end}}
{{- if eq .Comment "--"}} Because `--` starts a comment, write `a - -1` with a
space when subtracting a negative number.{{end}}

### Grouping and precedence

{{if eq .Prec "standard" -}}
Operators group the usual mathematical way. From tightest to loosest:
negation and `{{.Not}}`; then `*`, `/` and `{{.ModOp}}`; then `+` and `-`{{if ne .Concat "+"}} and `{{.Concat}}`{{end}}; then the comparisons; then
`{{.And}}`; then `{{.Or}}`. Operators of the same level group from left to
right, so `10 - 4 - 3` means `(10 - 4) - 3`.
{{- else if eq .Prec "flat-left" -}}
**All binary operators have the same precedence and group from left to
right.** There is no "multiplication first" rule: `2 + 3 * 4` means
`(2 + 3) * 4`. This applies to every binary operator, including comparisons
and `{{.And}}`/`{{.Or}}`, so `a < b {{.And}} c < d` means
`((a < b) {{.And}} c) < d`; write `(a < b) {{.And}} (c < d)` instead. Negation
and `{{.Not}}` bind tighter than any binary operator.
{{- else -}}
**All binary operators have the same precedence and group from right to
left.** There is no "multiplication first" rule: `2 * 3 + 4` means
`2 * (3 + 4)`, and `10 - 4 - 3` means `10 - (4 - 3)`. This applies to every
binary operator, including comparisons and `{{.And}}`/`{{.Or}}`, so
`a < b {{.And}} c < d` means `a < (b {{.And}} (c < d))`; write
`(a < b) {{.And}} (c < d)` instead. Negation and `{{.Not}}` bind tighter than
any binary operator.
{{- end}} Parentheses group explicitly.

{{template "ex" .Ex.prec}}

### Order of evaluation

{{if eq .Sem.OperandOrder "left-to-right" -}}
The two operands of a binary operator are evaluated left operand first.
{{- else -}}
**The two operands of a binary operator are evaluated right operand first**,
then the left one.
{{- end}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.operandorder}}

{{if eq .Sem.ArgOrder "left-to-right" -}}
Arguments of a function call are evaluated from left to right.
{{- else -}}
**Arguments of a function call are evaluated from right to left**: the last
argument first. They are still passed in the order written.
{{- end}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.argorder}}

### Integer arithmetic

Division of integers gives an integer.
{{- if eq .Sem.Div "toward-zero"}} The exact result is rounded toward zero,
so `7 / 2` is `3` and `-7 / 2` is `-3`.
{{- else if eq .Sem.Div "toward-negative-infinity"}} **The exact result is
rounded down, toward negative infinity**, so `7 / 2` is `3` and `-7 / 2` is `-4`.
{{- else}} **The exact result is rounded up, toward positive infinity**, so
`7 / 2` is `4` and `-7 / 2` is `-3`.
{{- end}} The remainder `a {{.ModOp}} b` is always `a - b * (a / b)` using that
division, so its sign follows from the rounding rule. Dividing by zero is an
error.

{{template "ex" .Ex.div}}

### Text

{{if eq .Concat "+" -}}
`+` adds two integers. If either operand is a string, both operands are
converted to text (as printing would show them) and joined instead. Note that
`1 + 2 + "x"` is `"3x"` but `"x" + 1 + 2` is `"x12"`, following the grouping
rules above.
{{- else -}}
`{{.Concat}}` converts both operands to text (as printing would show them)
and joins them. `+` only adds integers.
{{- end}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.concat}}

Strings can be compared with `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`, character by character
(by character code). Comparing values of different types with `{{.Eq}}` gives
`{{.False}}`; ordering them is an error.

### Lists and indexing

`x[i]` is element `i` of list `x`, or the one-character string at position
`i` of string `x`. **Indexes start at {{.Sem.IndexBase}}**: the first element
is `x[{{.Sem.IndexBase}}]`. List elements can be replaced with `x[i] {{.Assign}} value{{.Term}}`.

{{template "syntax" (dict . "index-assign")}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.index}}

{{if eq .Sem.OutOfRange "wrap" -}}
**An index outside the list wraps around**: it is taken modulo the length,
so one past the last element is the first element again, and negative
indexes count back from the end. Indexing an empty list or string is an
error.

{{template "ex" .Ex.wrap}}
{{- else -}}
Using an index outside the list or string is an error.
{{- end}}

{{if .Sem.ListEqStructural -}}
Two lists are equal (`{{.Eq}}`) when they have the same length and equal
elements.
{{- else -}}
**Two lists are equal (`{{.Eq}}`) only if they are the very same list.** Two
different lists with the same elements are not equal.
{{- end}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.listeq}}

### Logic and conditions

Conditions must be booleans: there is no "truthiness", and using an integer,
string, list or `{{.None}}` as a condition is an error. The operands of
`{{.And}}`, `{{.Or}}` and `{{.Not}}` must also be booleans.

{{if .Sem.ShortCircuit -}}
`{{.And}}` and `{{.Or}}` evaluate their right operand only when needed: if the
left operand of `{{.And}}` is `{{.False}}`, or the left operand of `{{.Or}}`
is `{{.True}}`, the right operand is not evaluated.
{{- else -}}
**`{{.And}}` and `{{.Or}}` always evaluate both operands**, even when the
left operand already decides the result.
{{- end}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.shortcircuit}}

## 6. Control flow

{{- if .Has.if}}

### Conditionals

{{template "syntax" (dict . "if")}}

The conditions are tested in order, and the statements of the first true
one run. If none is true, the statements of the final branch without a
condition run, if there is one.

{{template "ex" .Ex.if}}
{{- end}}

{{- if .Has.switch}}

### Choosing by value

{{template "syntax" (dict . "switch")}}

The expression is evaluated once and compared with each literal in order,
using `{{.Eq}}`. Literals are integers or strings, and must all be
different.
{{- if eq .Styles.Switch "c"}}
{{- if .Sem.SwitchFallthrough}} Execution starts at the first matching case
(or at the default part if none matches). **It then continues into the
following cases until `{{.Break}}`** or the end of the statement, so most
cases should end with `{{.Break}}{{.Term}}`.

{{template "ex" .Ex.fallthrough}}

The next example ends each case with `{{.Break}}`:
{{- else}} Only the statements of the matching case run (or the default part
if none matches); execution does not continue into the next case. `{{.Break}}` inside a case leaves the statement early.
{{- end}}
{{- else}} Only the statements of the matching case run (or the default part,
if none matches and there is one).
{{- if .Has.break}} `{{.Break}}` inside it refers to the enclosing loop, not
to this statement.{{end}}
{{- end}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.switch}}
{{- end}}

{{- if .Has.while}}

### While loops

{{template "syntax" (dict . "while")}}

The condition is tested before each pass; the loop ends when it is
`{{.False}}`.

{{template "ex" .Ex.while}}
{{- end}}

{{- if (index .Has "do-while")}}

### Do-while loops

{{template "syntax" (dict . "do-while")}}

The statements run once, then again for as long as the condition is
`{{.True}}`. The condition is tested after each pass.

{{template "ex" .Ex.posttest}}
{{- end}}

{{- if (index .Has "repeat-until")}}

### Repeat-until loops

{{template "syntax" (dict . "repeat-until")}}

The statements run once, then again **until** the condition is `{{.True}}`.
The condition is tested after each pass.

{{template "ex" .Ex.posttest}}
{{- end}}

{{- if (index .Has "c-for")}}

### For loops

{{template "syntax" (dict . "c-for")}}

The initializer runs once. Then, while the condition is `{{.True}}`, the
statements run, followed by the update.

{{template "ex" .Ex.cfor}}
{{- end}}

{{- if (index .Has "count-for")}}

### Counting loops

{{template "syntax" (dict . "count-for")}}

The start, limit and step are evaluated once, before the loop begins; the
step defaults to `1` and must not be `0`. The variable starts at the start
value and changes by the step after each pass.
{{- if .Sem.CountInclusive}} The loop runs while the variable has not passed the
limit; **the limit itself is included**.
{{- else}} **The limit itself is not included**: with a positive step, the loop
runs while the variable is less than the limit; with a negative step, while it
is greater.
{{- end}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.count}}
{{- end}}

{{- if .Has.break}}

### Leaving loops early

{{template "syntax" (dict . "break")}}
{{- if .Has.continue}}

{{template "syntax" (dict . "continue")}}

`{{.Break}}` leaves the innermost loop. `{{.Cont}}` skips to the next pass of
the innermost loop (for a counting or for loop, the update still happens).
{{- else}}

`{{.Break}}` leaves the switch statement it is in.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Has.continue}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.breakcont}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{- if .Has.label}}

### Labels and jumps

{{- if not .Has.if}}
{{.Name}} has no if statement; decisions are made with conditional jumps.
{{- end}}
{{- if not .Has.while}}
{{.Name}} has no loop statements; loops are written with labels and jumps.
{{- end}}

A label marks a position in a function. It does not end with `{{.Term}}`:

{{template "syntax" (dict . "label")}}

A jump continues execution at a label in the same function:

{{template "syntax" (dict . "goto")}}

A conditional jump jumps only if the condition is `{{.True}}`; otherwise
execution continues with the next statement:

{{template "syntax" (dict . "jumpif")}}

Each label name may be defined only once in a function. Jumps may go
forward or backward, and into or out of any block in the same function.
{{- if not .Has.while}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.labels}}
{{- else if not .Has.if}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.if}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{- if .Has.dispatch}}

### Dispatch

{{template "syntax" (dict . "dispatch")}}

The expression is evaluated once and compared with each literal in order,
using `{{.Eq}}`. On the first match, execution jumps to that literal's
label. If nothing matches, it jumps to the label after `{{.DispatchElse}}`, or,
without one, continues with the next statement. Literals are integers or
strings and must all be different.

{{template "ex" .Ex.switch}}
{{- end}}

## 7. Functions

{{template "syntax" (dict . "func")}}

Functions are declared at the top level of a program, not inside other
statements, and may be called before their declaration. Calling a function
with the wrong number of arguments is an error. Recursion is allowed.

{{template "syntax" (dict . "return")}}

`{{.Return}}` ends the function and gives back the value, or `{{.None}}`
without one. A function that ends without `{{.Return}}` returns `{{.None}}`.
A `{{.Return}}` in top-level code ends the program.

A call can be used as a statement, which discards its result:

{{template "syntax" (dict . "call")}}

{{template "ex" .Ex.func}}

## 8. Errors

Errors found before running, such as syntax errors, unknown names and wrong
argument counts, stop the program before anything runs. Errors found while
running stop it at that point. In both cases, the message names the line.

{{template "ex" .Ex.errors}}

